industrial water, wastewater, and other water and utility customers in Pennsylvania, Ohio, Texas, Illinois, North Carolina, New Jersey, Indiana, Virginia, and Kentucky under the Aqua and Peoples brands. The company was formerly known as Aqua America, Inc. and changed its name to Essential Utilities, Inc. in February 2020. Essential Utilities, Inc. was founded in 1886 and is headquartered in Bryn Mawr, Pennsylvania.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Christopher H. Franklin | Chairman, President & CEO | Age 62 | Compensation: $3.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 3K people. Revenue per employee of $749K ranks in the bottom quartile among Utilities peers, with revenue growing 9%/yr across its filed history. Stock-based compensation of $4K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Essential Utilities, Inc. is a large-cap utilities business with a $13.4B market cap on $2.5B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years and +12% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 6% against a 6% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 6.1\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 2.8\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.7% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 1.9\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 39% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.39322707333066337},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.1) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Regulatory rate-case risk and rising cost of capital \u2014 the allowed return is set by commissions, and the business lives or dies by that negotiation.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Grid modernisation capex burden \u2014 aging infrastructure requires massive reinvestment that regulators may not fully allow into rate base, compressing earned returns.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Wildfire and climate liability \u2014 extreme weather events create catastrophic, uninsurable losses that can push even investment-grade utilities toward restructuring.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 16% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","39 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Utilities sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 9% over 3 months"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Understanding Momentum Shifts in (WTRG)","source":"news.stocktradersdaily.com","link":"https://news.stocktradersdaily.com/news_release/35/Understanding_Momentum_Shifts_in_WTRG_112426082002_1795526402.html","date":"2026-11-24","timestamp":1795526402.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.0,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Global Water Resources (NASDAQ:GWRS) versus Essential Utilities (NYSE:WTRG) Head to Head Comparison","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/15/global-water-resources-nasdaqgwrs-versus-essential-utilities-nysewtrg-head-to-head-comparison.html","date":"2026-11-15","timestamp":1794767900.0,"origin":"tiingo",